# Application-specific Kconfig options

rsource "src/max32664_updater/Kconfig"
 
menu "HealthyPi Move application options"

config HPI_SH8601_HW_AOD
	bool "SH8601 native AOD mode for Always-on display"
	default y
	help
	  When enabled, Settings → Always-on uses the SH8601 panel AOD path
	  (AODMON 0x49 / AODMOFF 0x48 + AOD brightness 0x4A) after painting the
	  dim face. When disabled, Always-on falls back to software dimming of
	  normal display mode (lower power savings, useful for bring-up/A-B).

config HPI_GSR_SCREEN
		bool "Enable GSR screen"
		default y
		help
			Enable the Galvanic Skin Response (GSR) UI screens and related code.
			Disable to save flash/RAM and remove GSR UI. When disabled, GSR
			source files are excluded and runtime hooks become no-ops.

config HPI_GSR_STRESS_INDEX
		bool "Enable GSR stress index calculation"
		default y
		depends on HPI_GSR_SCREEN
		help
			Enable stress index calculation from GSR measurements including
			tonic/phasic separation, peak detection, and arousal scoring.
			Adds ~2KB flash and ~300 bytes RAM for history buffers.
			Disable to save memory if only raw GSR values are needed.

# Legacy CONFIG_HPI_RECORDING_MODULE (background multi-signal record + BLE/FS
# pull) was removed. Spot-check captures (ECG/GSR/HRV R-R) go through the health
# store record tier and sync via the HPI_HS MCUmgr group (docs/HPI_HS_API.md).

config HPI_IMU_MOTION_WAKE
	bool "BMI323 any-motion (wake-on-motion) interrupt"
	default n
	help
		Register the BMI323 any-motion interrupt (INT1 -> P1.12) at boot
		(P3 interrupt/RTIO). On motion the driver's trigger handler fires
		and the app records a motion event. Foundation for P4 wear-state
		power gating. OFF by default; enable to validate on device (boot
		log "BMI323 any-motion armed", then "any-motion detected" on move).

# --- HS-2 P1: health-store epoch aggregation -------------------------------
# Continuous signals are reduced to one epoch record per window instead of being
# written on every publish (~46k samples/day -> ~6k). The statistic emitted
# depends on the KIND of signal -- see docs/HS_SYNC_REDESIGN_PLAN.md 2.2.

config HPI_HS_EPOCH_HR_S
	int "Health-store HR epoch length (seconds)"
	default 60
	help
	  HR is spiky, so each epoch emits mean + min + max (the mean alone would
	  under-report peaks). Enable HPI_HS_EPOCH_SPREAD_LOG and check the logged
	  within-epoch spread on real wear before changing this: if the spread is
	  routinely small, this can grow to 300 and save ~5x more.

config HPI_HS_EPOCH_TEMP_S
	int "Health-store skin-temp epoch length (seconds)"
	default 300
	help
	  Skin temp moves over minutes, so each epoch emits mean + count only. No
	  min/max: a within-epoch extreme would capture artifacts (contact pressure,
	  sun, watch removal), not physiology, and a nightly min / daily max is
	  recoverable from the series of means.

config HPI_HS_EPOCH_COUNTER_S
	int "Health-store cumulative-counter epoch length (seconds)"
	default 60
	help
	  Steps and active energy are cumulative counters. Only the last value in the
	  window is meaningful (max == last; the mean of a monotonic ramp is not a
	  number that means anything). A decrease -- the local-midnight reset --
	  force-closes the window so the day's final total is not swallowed.

config HPI_HS_SYNTH
	bool "Health-store synthetic data generator (TEST ONLY)"
	default n
	help
	  Generates backdated, physiologically plausible data straight into the epoch
	  aggregator, so trends, the 7-day temp baseline, retention and sync-at-scale
	  can be tested WITHOUT wearing the watch for a week: it generates a week in
	  seconds.

	  Triggered by the HPI_HS `SYNTH` MCUmgr command (id 6) over BLE -- NOT at boot,
	  and not from a shell (this board's debug UART is TX-only, so nothing can be
	  typed into it). Boot-triggering was the old design and was a liability: a run
	  that faults partway comes back, regenerates and faults again -- a boot loop.

	  Every sample it writes carries HPI_HS_Q_SYNTHETIC so fabricated data can
	  never be mistaken for a real measurement. MUST BE OFF IN RELEASE BUILDS.

config HPI_STORAGE_LEGACY_SYNTH
	bool "Synthetic pre-3.0 storage tree (TEST ONLY)"
	default n
	help
	  Builds the directory tree a watch upgraded from 2.x carries
	  (/lfs/trhr, /lfs/trspo2, ... /lfs/log), so the one-shot purge in
	  hpi_storage_migrate.c can be exercised on a bench unit that was flashed
	  with 3.x and never had one.

	  Without this there is no way to test that purge at all, which is how a
	  batch of defects survived into review: a forward-progress bug in the
	  directory walker, a name-truncation hazard, and a revision stamped over a
	  tree that was still there. All three need a real tree to show up.

	  It also plants the two entries the walker has to cope with rather than
	  choke on -- a name too long for its batch buffer, and a stray
	  subdirectory -- and clears the migration stamp so the next boot purges.

	  Triggered by the HPI_HS LEGACY_SYNTH MCUmgr command (id 13) over BLE, not
	  at boot: a fixture rebuilt on every boot would race the purge it exists to
	  test. The command does not exist in a release image.

config HPI_HS_SYNTH_RTC_BOOTSTRAP
	bool "Set the RTC to a fixed test date if it is invalid (TEST ONLY)"
	default y
	depends on HPI_HS_SYNTH
	help
	  The synthetic generator needs a VALID RTC: hpi_sys_is_time_valid() requires a
	  2020..2030 timestamp, and SUMMARY computes "today" from the RTC, so synthetic
	  data timestamped outside the RTC's day would simply not show up.

	  A bench board typically has an unset RTC (it reads year 2000), and the debug
	  UART is TX-only so the time cannot be typed in. This sets a fixed date when the
	  RTC is invalid, which also makes the test dataset REPRODUCIBLE.

# --- HS-2 P3: continuous PPG-derived HRV ----------------------------------
# The MAX32664C already emits R-R intervals with a confidence value and nothing read
# them, so HRV/stress needed a manual ECG spot check. These gate the R-R stream.

config HPI_HS_HRV_WINDOW_S
	int "HRV window length (seconds)"
	default 300
	help
	  The Task-Force short-term HRV window. Metrics (RMSSD/SDNN/mean-RR/coverage) are
	  computed per window and stored; the raw beats are NOT (86,400 intervals/day would
	  be worse than the firehose P1 removed -- raw R-R belongs in the Record tier).

config HPI_HS_HRV_MIN_CONF
	int "Minimum hub R-R confidence to accept a beat (0..100)"
	default 80
	help
	  Motion destroys pulse-rate variability. A permissive gate does not produce more
	  HRV data -- it produces a plausible-looking trend made of artefacts, which is
	  worse than no trend at all. Lower this only with evidence.

config HPI_HS_HRV_QUIET_S
	int "Seconds of no IMU motion required before a beat is accepted"
	default 10
	help
	  The stillness gate, from the BMI323 any-motion trigger. 0 disables it (e.g. if
	  HPI_IMU_MOTION_WAKE is off), which leaves only the confidence + contact gates.

config HPI_HS_HRV_MIN_BEATS
	int "Minimum accepted beats before a window is emitted"
	default 30

config HPI_HS_HRV_MIN_COVERAGE
	int "Minimum window coverage (%) before a window is emitted"
	default 50
	help
	  Coverage = the share of the window actually backed by accepted beats. A window
	  below this is too sparse for its RMSSD to mean anything, so it is discarded
	  rather than stored as a misleading data point.
config HPI_HS_HRV_MIN_PAIRS
	int "Minimum accepted beat pairs before a window is emitted"
	default 10
	help
	  RMSSD is computed from the differences between successive beats. A window with
	  fewer than this number of pairs is too sparse for its RMSSD to mean anything,
	  so it is discarded rather than stored as a misleading data point.

config HPI_HS_EPOCH_SPREAD_LOG
	bool "Log within-epoch spread (max-min) for epoch-length validation"
	default n
	help
	  Prints n/mean/min/max/spread as each epoch closes. This is the measurement
	  that validates the epoch lengths above against real wear rather than
	  assumption. Leave off in production.
config HPI_HS_HRV_RR_RECORD
    bool "Enable raw HRV RR recording"
    default n
    help
      Store accepted HRV RR intervals as run-structured research data.
      Intended for research/debug builds only.
      Disabled by default because continuous RR recording generates
      significant storage traffic.
config HPI_HS_HRV_LOSS_LOG
	bool "Enable HRV rejection loss logging"
    default n
    help
      Enable per-window HRV rejection counters for bench debugging.
      The counters report rejected samples due to zero RTOR, low
      confidence, motion, no skin contact, or invalid RR range.
      This is bench telemetry only and is not stored as a health metric.
config HPI_HS_HRV_JITTER_SIGMA_MS_X10
    int "Beat-timing jitter sigma (ms x10) for RMSSD de-jittering"
    default 0
    help
      Measured beat-timing jitter standard deviation (sigma), in tenths of a
      millisecond. Used by hpi_hs_rmssd_dejitter() to subtract the noise
      floor: RMSSD_obs^2 = RMSSD_phys^2 + 6*sigma^2.

      Default 0 DISABLES correction entirely — this is the shipping default.
      Do not enable except with the sigma value produced by the REF-2 field
      experiment (see docs/STRESS_HRV_HANDOFF_REV2.md, H3).

      Measurement date: <fill in once REF-2 has run>
      Firmware SHA:     <fill in once REF-2 has run>
endmenu

# --- NCS v3.3 migration shim ---------------------------------------------
# With SB_CONFIG_SECURE_BOOT_NETCORE=y, NCS sysbuild (nrf/sysbuild/secureboot.cmake)
# pushes CONFIG_TFM_HAS_B0N into the default (app) image even on this secure
# `cpuapp` build that does not use TF-M. Upstream only defines TFM_HAS_B0N inside
# `if BUILD_WITH_TFM` (nrf/modules/trusted-firmware-m/Kconfig), and we blocklist
# trusted-firmware-m in west.yml, so the symbol is otherwise undefined here -- the
# sysbuild assignment then aborts Kconfig ("assign to undefined symbol"). Define an
# inert stub (mirrors the upstream prompt) purely to absorb that informative value.
# Nothing in this non-TF-M build reads it.
config TFM_HAS_B0N
	bool "Network core bootloader present (informative only, do not change)"
	depends on SOC_SERIES_NRF53
	help
	  Migration stub mirroring nrf/modules/trusted-firmware-m/Kconfig. Absorbs the
	  sysbuild-propagated CONFIG_TFM_HAS_B0N value on no-TF-M secure-boot builds.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
